questions.model_answer:
The LRM guarantees constant-time access to any element and constant-time insertion and removal at either end of a queue (§7.10). It does not specify the cost of inserting or deleting in the middle; in typical implementations that moves elements and grows with the queue size, but that is implementation behaviour, not a language rule.

The real problem here is the search: `find_first_index` scans the queue, so each observed transaction costs time proportional to the number outstanding. With N outstanding, total work grows roughly as N squared as traffic grows.

Restructure by key:

```systemverilog
my_txn exp_by_id[int][$];        // associative array of queues: ID -> in-order list

function void add_expected(my_txn t);
  exp_by_id[t.id].push_back(t);
endfunction

function void check_actual(my_txn t);
  if (!exp_by_id.exists(t.id) || exp_by_id[t.id].size() == 0)
    $error("unexpected id %0d", t.id);
  else begin
    my_txn e = exp_by_id[t.id].pop_front();   // same-ID order is preserved
    if (!e.compare(t)) $error("mismatch id %0d", t.id);
    if (exp_by_id[t.id].size() == 0) exp_by_id.delete(t.id);
  end
endfunction
```

Lookup is now by key (associative arrays are typically hashed or tree-based; the exact cost is implementation-dependent), and per-ID ordering, which protocols such as AXI require, is kept. Delete empty keys so the table does not grow forever, and report leftovers at the end of the test.

