questions.model_answer:
At each edge, `@(posedge clk)` in the testbench and the DUT's `always_ff @(posedge clk)` both wake in the Active region, and the LRM lets the simulator run them in any order (§4.7). If the testbench runs first, its blocking write of `valid` is visible when the flop evaluates `v_q <= valid`, so the DUT captures at edge 2 (trace A). If the DUT runs first, it reads the old 0 and captures one edge later (trace B). Neither tool is wrong; the bench is.

A clocking block fixes both directions:

```systemverilog
clocking cb @(posedge clk);
  default input #1step output #0;
  output valid;
  input  ready;
endclocking
// driver: @(cb); cb.valid <= 1;
```

- Drives (`cb.valid <= 1`) with zero output skew are scheduled in the Re-NBA region (§14.16), after every design process for this edge has run, so the DUT sees the new value at the *next* edge on every simulator.

- Inputs with the default `#1step` skew return the value from the Postponed region of the previous time step, which is the same value Preponed sampling sees (§14.3, §14.13, §4.4.2.1). The bench reads what a flop would capture.

- `@(cb)` triggers in the Observed region (§14.10), after the design's NBA updates, so code that resumes on it runs after the RTL has settled.

Reading DUT outputs at a raw `@(posedge clk)` is deterministic only while every path to them is written with nonblocking assignments. The clocking block makes it deterministic regardless.

