```systemverilog
interface vr_if #(int W = 8) (input logic clk, input logic rst_n);
  logic [W-1:0] data;
  logic         valid, ready;

  modport master (input clk, rst_n, ready, output data, valid);
  modport slave  (input clk, rst_n, data, valid, output ready);
  modport mon    (input clk, rst_n, data, valid, ready);
endinterface

module sink (vr_if.slave bus);
  always_ff @(posedge bus.clk) bus.ready <= 1'b1;
endmodule

module top;
  logic clk, rst_n;
  vr_if #(.W(8)) bus (.clk(clk), .rst_n(rst_n));
  sink u_sink (.bus(bus));       // connects through the slave modport
endmodule
```

An interface bundles signals (and optionally tasks, functions, clocking blocks and assertions) into one port (§25.3). A modport is a view of that bundle: it lists which signals a module may see and in which direction (§25.5). It does not create storage or copy signals: `bus.ready` in `sink` is the same variable as `top.bus.ready`. Driving a signal that the modport declares as `input` is an error, so modports document and enforce who drives what.

Practical points:

- Put the clock and reset on the interface ports so every instance is driven by the top (§25.4).

- Add a clocking block and include it in the testbench modport, so drivers use `bus.cb.data <= ...` race-free.

- Put the handshake itself in the interface. A task or function declared in the interface is made available to a module through a modport `import` (for example `modport master (..., import send);`), so every master runs the same protocol code instead of its own copy (§25.7).

- A class reaches the same instance through a virtual interface, optionally typed with a modport (`virtual vr_if.master`), which is null until assigned (§25.9).

