questions.model_answer:
It prints `p1.len=4 p1.h.id=2`. `new p1` is a shallow copy: it allocates a new `pkt` and copies every property, but for class-handle properties it copies the handle, not the object. `p1.h` and `p2.h` refer to the same `hdr`, even though the class declaration contains `= new()` (§8.12). The shallow copy also does not call the constructor or run declaration initializers (§8.12).

Deep copy:

```systemverilog
class hdr;
  bit [7:0] id;
  function hdr copy();
    copy = new();
    copy.id = id;
  endfunction
endclass

class pkt;
  bit [7:0] len;
  hdr       h = new();
  virtual function pkt copy();
    copy     = new();
    copy.len = len;
    copy.h   = (h == null) ? null : h.copy();   // new object, not the handle
  endfunction
endclass
```

Make the copy method `virtual` and override it in subclasses, so copying through a base handle copies the whole derived object (a polymorphic clone). In UVM, implement `do_copy` (calling `super.do_copy`) and use `clone()`, which returns `uvm_object` and needs a `$cast`.

The scoreboard version of the bug: a monitor creates one transaction object, refills it every cycle and writes the same handle to its analysis port. Analysis ports pass the handle, not a copy, so every entry in the scoreboard's queue is the same object, and all "expected" items change when the next transaction is sampled. Fix: create a new object per observed transaction in the monitor (preferred), or clone in the subscriber before storing.

