questions.model_answer:
Abstract class (§8.21): a `virtual class` cannot be constructed directly; it can hold state and implemented methods next to `pure virtual` prototypes that every non-abstract subclass must implement. A class can extend only one base.

```systemverilog
virtual class ref_model_base #(type T = int);
  protected int unsigned n_seen;           // shared state
  function void observe(T t);              // template method
    n_seen++;
    predict(t);
  endfunction
  pure virtual protected function void predict(T t);
endclass
```

Interface class (§8.26): contains only `pure virtual` methods, type declarations and parameters; no data, constraints or covergroups. A class can implement several interface classes in addition to extending one base.

```systemverilog
interface class resettable;
  pure virtual function void on_reset();
endclass

interface class reportable;
  pure virtual function string summary();
endclass

class my_model extends ref_model_base #(bus_txn) implements resettable, reportable;
  virtual protected function void predict(bus_txn t); /* ... */ endfunction
  virtual function void on_reset();  n_seen = 0;                  endfunction
  virtual function string summary(); return $sformatf("%0d", n_seen); endfunction
endclass
```

Choosing:

- Use an abstract base when implementations share state or a fixed algorithm (the template method above), and when there is one natural "is-a" hierarchy, as with UVM components, which must extend `uvm_component`.

- Use interface classes for capabilities that cut across hierarchies: an object that is already a `uvm_component` or `uvm_object` can still promise `resettable` or `reportable`. A VIP can then hold a `resettable` handle and call `on_reset()` on anything that implements it, whatever its base class.

- Interface-class handles can be cast to and from implementing class handles with `$cast` (§8.26.5).

Trade-offs: interface classes cannot carry default behaviour, so every implementer repeats boilerplate; abstract classes lock users into one base. Many VIP APIs combine them: an abstract base for the common case and interface classes for optional capabilities.

