```systemverilog
task automatic run_channels(int n, time limit);
  process workers[int];                  // keyed by channel, so order of start does not matter
  int     started = 0;

  for (int ch = 0; ch < n; ch++) begin
    fork
      automatic int k = ch;
      begin
        workers[k] = process::self();
        started++;
        run_channel(k);
      end
    join_none
  end
  wait (started == n);                    // every worker has registered itself

  #limit;
  foreach (workers[k]) begin
    process::state st;
    st = workers[k].status();
    if (st != process::FINISHED) begin
      $error("channel %0d still %s at timeout", k, st.name());
      workers[k].kill();                // also kills that worker's own children
    end
  end
endtask
```

How it works (§9.7): `process::self()` returns a handle to the calling process. `status()` reports FINISHED, RUNNING, WAITING, SUSPENDED or KILLED. `kill()` terminates that process and all its descendants. `await()` waits for another process to end (not for itself). `suspend()`/`resume()` pause and restart a process. You cannot construct a `process` with `new` or extend the class.

Why not the alternatives:

- `disable fork` kills every descendant of the calling process (§9.6.3): all workers, finished or not, plus any monitors this task forked earlier. It cannot kill a selected subset.

- `disable <label>` works on the static block name, so it ends every process executing that block, in all calls of the task (§9.6.2).

- The process handle gives per-thread status for the report, which neither alternative provides.

Details that matter: registering into an associative array keyed by channel avoids depending on the order in which the forked processes start. The `wait (started == n)` guard prevents dereferencing a handle that has not been set yet: forked processes only start once the parent blocks. A process can also be seeded with `srandom()`, so each channel's random stream stays stable when channels are added (§9.7, §18.14).

